{"name":"Palm","hex":"#afaf5e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Palm","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 15924  script code for Palmyrene."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname mainly of Scandinavian origin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Palm"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hand","blocks":[{"text":"A hand is a prehensile, multi-fingered appendage located at the distal end (extremity) of the forelimb or upper limb of primates such as humans, chimpanzees, monkeys, and lemurs. A few other arboreal vertebrates such as the koala are often described as having \"hands\" instead of paws on their front limbs. The raccoon is usually described as having \"hands\" though opposable thumbs are lacking."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Hand%2C_fingers_-_back.jpg/330px-Hand%2C_fingers_-_back.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hand","wikidata":"Q33767"},{"title":"Arecaceae","blocks":[{"text":"The Arecaceae are a family of perennial, flowering plants in the monocot order Arecales. Their growth form can be climbers, shrubs, tree-like and stemless plants, all commonly known as palms. Those having a tree-like form are colloquially called palm trees. Currently, 181 genera with around 2,600 species are known, most of which are restricted to tropical and subtropical climates. Most palms are distinguished by their large, compound, evergreen leaves, known as fronds, arranged at the top of an unbranched stem, except for the Hyphaene genus, which has branched palms. However, palms exhibit an enormous diversity in physical characteristics and inhabit nearly every type of habitat within their range, from rainforests to deserts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/1859-Martinique.web.jpg/330px-1859-Martinique.web.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link"